Gnome Warlock Fizzwidget, perched atop a cliff in Stranglethorn, watched his succubus, Velvet, charm a panther with a sultry whisper. "Seduce it, darling," he purred, sipping gnomish tea. The panther nuzzled Velvet, oblivious. Just as Fizzwidget began his Drain Life, a rogue tiger pounced—aggro’d by a wandering troll hunter. Velvet shrieked, breaking charm. The panther turned, and now *two* beasts snapped at Fizzwidget’s ankles. He tried Soothing Kiss, but threat had already spiked. His health plummeted. No taunt. No shield. No out-of-combat regen after. He died cursing, resurrected by a passing shaman who raised an eyebrow at his foolish, beautiful pet.

**Answer:** Voidwalkers generate threat (Torment, Suffering) and provide survivability (Sacrifice shield, Consume Shadows for OOC regen), making them safer for solo questing—especially when pulling multiple mobs or needing downtime recovery. Succubus offers utility (Seduce, invis) but lacks reliable threat or healing, risking pet loss or death if crowd control breaks or aggro slips. Thus, warlocks favor voidwalkers for consistent, low-risk leveling. [end of text]


